  "reading": "The flag is worth 1.5x to 2.0x while the checkpoint fits in RAM comfortably (8B warm 6.78 -> 4.49 is the low end, 12B warm 4.96 -> 2.51 the high end) and 7.5x when it does not (31B cold 88.52 -> 11.75). vllm#49991 claimed a flat 3.9x to 5.6x and called that magnitude permanent; that figure came from the uncontrolled 2026-07-28 run and does not reproduce. The resident-set split gives the mechanism directly rather than by inference: on the 31B the default path ends with 21 390 MiB of RssAnon against 782 MiB of RssFile, and with the flag those swap places, 843 against 21 479. The copy-on-write break converts the whole checkpoint into private dirty memory, which is why a checkpoint that is a large fraction of RAM pushes the loader into swap and why the penalty is not a constant ratio. Sharding bounds it: Qwen3-8B is the same 15.26 GiB in five shards and peaks at 4 535 MiB of RssAnon, roughly one shard. The end-to-end vllm serve figures track the loader-path ones closely (12B warm 4.59/4.60 -> 2.55/2.58, cold 7.32/7.90 -> 4.93/5.11), so the isolated harness is not flattering itself.",
  "against_the_flag": "On the 128-expert MoE checkpoint the flag is no better than doing nothing and possibly slightly worse, 19.95 against 19.17 with overlapping ranges, because almost nothing there is large enough to pay the copy-on-write cost and the extra host copy is pure overhead. vllm#49991 says cloning was faster in every configuration measured and that default-on looked sensible; this is the counterexample, and that sentence has to go. safetensors 0.8.0 also ships backend=\"pread\", undocumented in the Python docstring but named in the v0.8.0 release notes, which never maps the file at all: it is slower than the clone everywhere here except the MoE checkpoint, where it is the fastest of the four, and its peak RSS is 2.4-3.9 GiB against 4.9-21.8 for every other path. It returned byte-identical tensors for all 1334 tensors of a 12B shard.",
  "not_established": "The 2026-07-28 'healthy kernel' column does not reproduce on -30 even when its ordering is replayed with no cache control (8B baseline 13.87 s against the 36.4 s reported then, 12B 9.73 against 18.5, 31B 126.61 s against a >900 s timeout). Page cache is therefore not the whole explanation. Two things changed between the sessions: the kernel state (a hand-built -28 module carrying 342981fff328 against Canonical's -30) and this VM's PCIe atomics, which were off in July and on now. The 32 MiB reproducer moved only 17.0 -> 15.3 ms across the same change, so the per-copy path is not where the difference lives, but nothing here identifies where it does. The July table is superseded, not reconciled. Everything here is one machine, one VFIO guest, gfx1100 only; CDNA is untested, and no measurement here says what the flag is worth on a host whose RAM dwarfs the checkpoint."
